A reference must bind to a variable instantly and cannot switch its allegiance:

int x = 10;
int y = 20;

int& ref = x;      // ref is now an alias for x. Must be initialized!
ref = 15;          // Direct access (implicitly dereferenced). x becomes 15.

ref = y;           // This does NOT change ref to point to y. 
                   // Instead, it copies y's value into x (x becomes 20).

A static member is a class member that belongs to the class itself, rather than to individual objects.

It is created only once, and all objects of that class share the same static member.

class Student {
    int rollNo;                  // instance variable
    static String college = "ABC College";  // static variable

    Student(int rollNo) {
        this.rollNo = rollNo;
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.println(rollNo + " " + college);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Student s1 = new Student(101);
        Student s2 = new Student(102);

        s1.display();
        s2.display();
    }
}

